Patterns: Gamification

Purpose:
  Reusable patterns for gamification systems.
  Covers player identity, points economy, badges, leaderboards,
  event-driven actions, rule engines, anti-cheat, and real-time delivery.

Owns: ./

Patterns:

  PlayerIdentity:
    name: Player Identity
    description: Stable external identity for gamification platforms
    complexity: Low
    scale_fit: L0-L5
    pros:
      - decouples internal user model from external platform
      - supports multiple external gamification providers
      - enables user migration and A/B testing
    cons:
      - requires identity mapping maintenance
      - eventual consistency between internal and external profiles
    when_to_use:
      - any external gamification integration
      - multi-provider strategies
      - user migration scenarios
    when_to_avoid:
      - single internal gamification system
    best_practices:
      - use immutable external ID (auid) as primary reference
      - maintain bidirectional ID mapping with TTL
      - sync profile data asynchronously with backoff
      - cache player profile with invalidation on update
      - never expose internal IDs to external platform

  PointsEconomy:
    name: Points Economy
    description: Event-driven point awards with idempotency and audit
    complexity: Medium
    scale_fit: L1-L5
    pros:
      - transparent point flow
      - supports complex earning rules
      - enables analytics and fraud detection
    cons:
      - requires idempotency and deduplication
      - eventual consistency for aggregated balances
      - audit log storage overhead
    when_to_use:
      - any points-based gamification
      - multiple point sources
      - compliance requirements
    when_to_avoid:
      - simple single-source points
      - no audit requirements
    best_practices:
      - award points via events, not direct DB writes
      - make point events idempotent (event_id dedup)
      - maintain immutable audit trail for all point changes
      - separate point ledger from read model (CQRS)
      - enforce daily earning limits per user and per action
      - batch point aggregation for balance queries
      - use optimistic locking for concurrent point updates

  BadgeAchievement:
    name: Badge and Achievement
    description: Rule-based badge unlocking with status tracking
    complexity: Medium
    scale_fit: L1-L5
    pros:
      - flexible rule definitions
      - supports complex unlock conditions
      - enables dynamic badge campaigns
    cons:
      - rule evaluation overhead
      - requires careful state management
      - time-based rules need scheduler
    when_to_use:
      - any achievement system
      - dynamic badge campaigns
      - progression tracking
    when_to_avoid:
      - static single-badge systems
      - no progression requirements
    best_practices:
      - evaluate badges asynchronously after point events
      - make badge rules declarative (JSON config)
      - support time-window rules (daily, weekly, all-time)
      - track badge status: locked, unlocked, expired, revoked
      - batch badge evaluation for offline users
      - cache unlocked badges per user with TTL
      - emit events on badge unlock for real-time notification

  Leaderboard:
    name: Leaderboard
    description: Ranked player lists with multiple scopes and caching
    complexity: Medium-High
    scale_fit: L2-L5
    pros:
      - engaging competitive feature
      - supports multiple ranking dimensions
      - enables social features
    cons:
      - expensive to maintain in real-time
      - requires careful caching strategy
      - tie-breaking complexity
    when_to_use:
      - competitive or social features
      - multiple ranking scopes (global, friends, regional)
      - high user volume
    when_to_avoid:
      - low user count
      - no competitive element
      - simple personal dashboards only
    best_practices:
      - separate write model (point events) from read model (leaderboard)
      - use sorted sets (Redis) or materialized views for hot leaderboards
      - batch recalculate stale leaderboards asynchronously
      - support multiple scopes: global, friends, regional, time-window
      - implement tie-breaking: secondary sort by timestamp, then auid
      - cache top-N results with short TTL (30s-5m)
      - paginate deep leaderboards; avoid full scans
      - snapshot leaderboards for historical/seasonal views

  EventDrivenActions:
    name: Event-Driven Actions
    description: User actions captured as events for gamification processing
    complexity: Medium
    scale_fit: L1-L5
    pros:
      - decouples action capture from processing
      - supports replay and re-processing
      - enables audit and analytics
    cons:
      - requires event infrastructure
      - ordering challenges at scale
      - storage overhead for event history
    when_to_use:
      - any action-tracking gamification
      - multi-platform event sources
      - audit requirements
    when_to_avoid:
      - simple single-action systems
      - no replay requirements
    best_practices:
      - emit events at action source (client, API, webhook)
      - include actor, action, context, timestamp in event envelope
      - use correlation ID to link action to point/badge outcomes
      - batch events for processing (1s-5s windows)
      - deduplicate at ingestion layer (event_id or auid + action + time window)
      - persist raw events for replay and debugging
      - support both real-time (WebSocket) and batch (queue) ingestion

  RuleEngine:
    name: Gamification Rule Engine
    description: Declarative rules for points, badges, and conditions
    complexity: Medium-High
    scale_fit: L2-L5
    pros:
      - non-developers can create rules
      - supports A/B testing and campaigns
      - enables rapid iteration
    cons:
      - rule evaluation complexity
      - requires validation and testing framework
      - performance overhead for complex rules
    when_to_use:
      - dynamic gamification campaigns
      - multiple rule variants
      - non-technical rule authors
    when_to_avoid:
      - static hardcoded rules
      - simple single-condition systems
    best_practices:
      - define rules in declarative format (JSON/YAML)
      - support conditions: user_attr, action_count, time_window, streak
      - support actions: award_points, unlock_badge, notify
      - version rules and support gradual rollout
      - validate rules before activation (syntax, logic, performance)
      - log rule evaluation decisions for debugging
      - support rule chaining and dependencies
      - implement rule timeout to prevent infinite loops

  AntiCheat:
    name: Anti-Cheat
    description: Validation and anomaly detection for gamification integrity
    complexity: High
    scale_fit: L2-L5
    pros:
      - maintains system integrity
      - enables fair competition
      - supports automated rollback
    cons:
      - requires monitoring and tuning
      - false positive risk
      - performance overhead
    when_to_use:
      - any competitive gamification
      - real-money or high-value rewards
      - public leaderboards
    when_to_avoid:
      - purely decorative gamification
      - no external validation possible
    best_practices:
      - enforce rate limits per user per action (min/max interval)
      - validate action context: IP, device, session, time-of-day
      - detect statistical anomalies (z-score, clustering)
      - maintain rollback capability for point adjustments
      - log all suspicious activity with evidence
      - implement progressive penalties: warn, cap, revoke, ban
      - separate detection (async) from enforcement (async)
      - use human review for high-value rollbacks

  RealTimeDelivery:
    name: Real-Time Delivery
    description: WebSocket-based real-time gamification updates
    complexity: Medium
    scale_fit: L1-L5
    pros:
      - engaging user experience
      - immediate feedback
      - supports live features
    cons:
      - connection management overhead
      - requires fallback for disconnected users
      - scaling challenges at high concurrency
    when_to_use:
      - live leaderboards
      - real-time point notifications
      - multiplayer or social features
    when_to_avoid:
      - offline-first applications
      - low real-time requirements
    best_practices:
      - use WebSocket firehose with topic-based subscriptions
      - implement heartbeat and auto-reconnect with exponential backoff
      - buffer events for briefly disconnected clients
      - fall back to polling when WebSocket unavailable
      - batch updates to reduce message frequency
      - compress large payloads (leaderboard snapshots)
      - implement backpressure handling
      - authenticate connections at upgrade time

  CQRSGamification:
    name: CQRS for Gamification
    description: Separate read and write models for gamification data
    complexity: Medium-High
    scale_fit: L2-L5
    pros:
      - optimized read/write paths
      - scalable leaderboard queries
      - supports multiple read models
    cons:
      - eventual consistency complexity
      - more infrastructure
      - requires sync mechanisms
    when_to_use:
      - high read/write ratio
      - multiple query patterns
      - scaling bottlenecks
    when_to_avoid:
      - simple low-traffic systems
      - strong consistency required everywhere
    best_practices:
      - write model: immutable events (point_awarded, badge_unlocked)
      - read model: materialized views (player_balance, leaderboard, badge_grid)
      - sync via event handlers with idempotent projections
      - support multiple read models from same event stream
      - rebuild read models from events when corrupted
      - cache read models with appropriate TTL per use case

Selection:
  AI MUST select gamification patterns based on:
    ProjectScale (L0-L5)
    User volume
    Competitive features required
    Real-time requirements
    Compliance and audit requirements
    Team capability
    Infrastructure constraints

Navigation:
  Architecture: @../architecture/INDEX.sdd
  Skills: @../../skills/sdd/INDEX.sdd
  Project: @../project/INDEX.sdd

Rules:
  [GAM-01] Player identity MUST use immutable external ID (auid) as primary reference.
  [GAM-02] Point awards MUST be event-driven and idempotent.
  [GAM-03] Badge rules MUST be declarative and versioned.
  [GAM-04] Leaderboards MUST separate write model from read model.
  [GAM-05] Real-time delivery MUST have polling fallback.
  [GAM-06] Anti-cheat MUST be async and non-blocking.
  [GAM-07] All point and badge changes MUST be auditable.
  [GAM-08] Daily limits MUST be enforced per user and per action type.
  [GAM-09] Rule evaluation MUST be bounded with timeout.
  [GAM-10] Rollback capability MUST exist for fraudulent point adjustments.
  [GAM-11] WebSocket connections MUST be authenticated and heartbeat-monitored.
  [GAM-12] Leaderboard tie-breaking MUST be deterministic.
  [GAM-13] Player profile sync MUST be asynchronous with backoff.
  [GAM-14] Event ingestion MUST deduplicate before processing.
  [GAM-15] Gamification data MUST be classified (PUBLIC, INTERNAL, CONFIDENTIAL).

State: +